an emotionally charged gathering outside  the supreme court of honduras  many hope to catch a glimpse of former  honduran president juan orlando  hernandez wanted by u.s authorities on  international drug trafficking and  illegal weapons charges  hernandez arrived in handcuffs and  chains for a preliminary court hearing  related to an extradition request filed  by the united states earlier this week  we are here to celebrate we're here to  celebrate the narco dictatorship that is  failing today with its greatest leader  tensions outside the court led to minor  scuffles between critics and supporters  of the former president  we are supporting our president in peace  so a dignified process is carried out  and his rights are respected we are also  supporting him peacefully as the  national party as leaders of the party  we are not looking for trouble we are  not attacking anyone we just want our  president to realise he's not alone  hernandez was arrested on tuesday at his  home in the honduran capital u.s  prosecutors allege hernandez helped  facilitate the trafficking of hundreds  of tons of narcotics from colombia and  venezuela over the course of his eight  years as president allegations the 53  year old former hunter and leader has  repeatedly  denied his fate now in the hands of a  judge overseeing a request for  extradition by the united states  in order to guarantee his presence at  the extradition proceedings he will be  provisionally detained at the facilities  of the special operations command of the  national police  federal prosecutors in new york had  previously named hernandez a  co-conspirator in a major international  drug trafficking case  and last year a u.s judge sentenced  president hernandez's younger brother  tony hernandez to life in prison over  charges related to cocaine trafficking  having concluded the first of two  appearances before the supreme court  former honduran president juan orlando  hernandez will remain under the custody  of security forces until march 16th for  a second and final extradition hearing  manuel rapalo al jazeera tegucigalpa
 

